Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can serialize a `RootObjectMapper` to XContent **with** the default settings for:

| Setting | What it does |
|---------|--------------|
| `dynamicDateFormats` | The list of date formats that the mapper will try when parsing dates (e.g. `"strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"`). |
| `dynamicTemplates` | Templates that are applied to dynamically added fields. |
| `dateDetection` | Enables automatic detection of date fields. |
| `numericDetection` | Enables automatic detection of numeric fields. |

The example also demonstrates how to **skip runtime fields** during serialization.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.RootObjectMapper;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.ArrayList;

// 1. Build the RootObjectMapper with the desired defaults
RootObjectMapper rootMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder("my_index")
        // Default date formats – this is the same value that Elasticsearch uses internally
        .dynamicDateFormats(Arrays.asList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"))
        // Example dynamic template – you can add as many as you need
        .dynamicTemplates(Arrays.asList(
                new RootObjectMapper.DynamicTemplate("string_as_keyword",
                        new RootObjectMapper.DynamicTemplate.MatchMappingType("string"),
                        new RootObjectMapper.DynamicTemplate.Mapping("keyword"))))
        // Enable date and numeric detection
        .dateDetection(true)
        .numericDetection(true)
        .build();

// 2. Create an XContentBuilder instance (JSON in this case)
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();

// 3. Prepare the parameters for the doXContent call
//    - First element: includeDefaults (true)
//    - Second element: skipRuntimeFields (true)
ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // includeDefaults
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // skipRuntimeFields

// 4. Call the function that serializes the mapper
//    (the function signature expects *string* representations of the arguments)
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
        new String[]{"builder", "params"},
        new String[]{builder.toString(), params.toString()}
);

// 5. Inspect the resulting JSON
System.out.println(builder.string());
```

### What the function call actually does

```java
functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
        new String[]{"builder", "params"},
        new String[]{builder.toString(), params.toString()}
);
```

* `builder.toString()` – a string representation of the `XContentBuilder` instance you created in step 2.  
* `params.toString()` – a string representation of the `ArrayList` that contains the two boolean flags (`includeDefaults` and `skipRuntimeFields`).  

The `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` function will write the mapper’s configuration into the builder, honouring the flags you passed. Because `skipRuntimeFields` is `true`, any runtime fields that might exist on the mapper will be omitted from the output.

### Result

Running the code above will print a JSON representation similar to:

```json
{
  "_source": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  "dynamic": "strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis",
  "dynamic_templates": [
    {
      "string_as_keyword": {
        "match_mapping_type": "string",
        "mapping": {
          "type": "keyword"
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "date_detection": true,
  "numeric_detection": true
}
```

Feel free to adjust the `dynamicTemplates` list or other settings to match your exact mapping requirements.
